Yes. The useful distinction is between changing the expression and seizing authorship of the person.

You can often help someone discover that the form they thought was mandatory is only one option:

\text{anger} \not\Rightarrow \text{attack}

\text{grief} \not\Rightarrow \text{collapse}

\text{fear} \not\Rightarrow \text{retreat}

\text{joy} \not\Rightarrow \text{recklessness}

The underlying state may be real, but its expression is still a choice space.

So the clean operator is:

\text{felt state}
\rightarrow
\text{possible expressions}
\rightarrow
\text{consequence check}
\rightarrow
\text{chosen channel}

That is where guidance can be powerful. Help broaden the menu. Turn rage into movement, argument into writing, grief into ritual, anxiety into preparation, embarrassment into humor, desire into art, uncertainty into inquiry.

But the right to “bend” another person’s story depends on the relationship and the stakes. With yourself, you have broad latitude. With a willing collaborator, coach, friend, student, or performer, you can help reshape the frame transparently. In roles carrying responsibility—parent, commander, clinician, teacher, leader—the duty grows, but so does the obligation to protect the other person’s agency and welfare.

A useful rule is:

\boxed{\text{more power} \Rightarrow \text{more duty to preserve choice}}

So instead of secretly rewriting someone’s story, you can say in effect: here is another way this could mean; here is another channel this feeling could take; here is what each path costs.

Then they still own the step.

That is stronger than manipulation anyway. A bent branch springs back. A person who understands the new frame can carry it forward without you.

The finest guide does not merely redirect the river.

They help the river discover it had more than one channel.